<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Concordat of Worms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1122">1122</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Pactum Calixtinum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Concordat of Worms was the agreement of September 1122 between Emperor Henry V and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-ii">Pope Callixtus II</a> that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a>. The emperor gave up investing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> with ring and staff, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> elections were to take place in his presence and he granted the elected bishop the regalia with a sceptre. It was concluded at Worms, one of the leading cities of the Salian emperors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/concordat-of-worms.jpg" alt="Concordat of Worms" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Original uploader was DALIBRI at de.wikipedia; derivative work by Saibo (Δ) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urkunde_Wormser_Konkordat-bg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> began in the middle of the 11th century, when a reforming movement in the Church sought to reassert the rights of the Holy See. Pope Gregory VII forbade secular rulers to make clerical appointments, and Emperor Henry IV, who habitually invested the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots of the Empire, rejected the claim. The dispute outlasted both men. In 1111 Henry V made an agreement with Pope Paschal II at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a>, but when it was not carried out he imprisoned the pope, who then yielded under duress and later went back on his promises.</p><p>Callixtus II resumed talks, and in autumn 1119 the emperor agreed in principle at Strasbourg to give up investing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots with ring and crosier. A planned summit at Mouzon broke down, and Henry was excommunicated at the council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. Afterwards most of the clergy became willing to compromise. In 1121 a faction of nobles led by Archbishop Adalbert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> pressed Henry to make peace, and in February 1122 Callixtus wrote to him in a conciliatory tone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The talks seem to have taken place at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> while Henry waited at Worms. The papal delegation was led by Cardinal Bishop Lamberto Scannabecchi of Ostia, the future Pope Honorius II. On 23 September 1122 delegates signed the documents outside the walls of Worms, which had no room for the crowd. The agreement was issued as two charters, the Calixtinum, addressed to the emperor, and the Henricianum, addressed to God. Neither was signed.</p><p>Henry renounced investiture with ring and staff, opened ecclesiastical appointments in his realm to canonical election, recognised the papal patrimony and promised to return church lands he or his father had seized. Callixtus allowed the emperor or his officials to be present at elections and to judge disputed ones, and let him invest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots with a sceptre representing their temporal lands. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> this came before consecration, elsewhere in the Empire within six months. The charters were deliberately brief, and the word regalia meant different things to each side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The First Lateran Council confirmed the concordat in March 1123 with nearly 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and 600 abbots present. Some followers of Paschal II cried non placet, and Callixtus told them that they had not to approve but to tolerate it. Henry V died in 1125. Historians disagree about how decisive the settlement was. Some stress that it ended the Ottonian and Salian imperial church system and showed that the emperor held no religious authority; others note that the king was left in possession of the field and that the controversy over investiture continued for at least another decade. Successive emperors found its terms favourable enough that it remained almost unaltered until the Empire was dissolved in 1806.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Concordat of Worms” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the German lands the princes elected the king, who sought coronation as emperor in Rome, and the long quarrel with the popes over the appointment of bishops, settled by the <b>Concordat of Worms</b> in 1122, weakened his office.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Innocent III excommunicated the Holy Roman Emperor Otto IV, who had demanded that he annul the <b>Concordat of Worms</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-18">18 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its citizens won growing privileges from the Salian emperors, who signed the <b>Concordat of Worms</b> there in 1122.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-worms">Free Imperial City of Worms</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops lost control of Worms itself, which became a free imperial city; the <b>Concordat of Worms</b> in 1122 and Luther's appearance at the Diet of Worms in 1521 took place there.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-worms">Prince-Bishopric of Worms</a></li></ul></section>
